Clay-covered areas
Soil properties for one of the hydrogeological environments described in Lundmark, A., 
2008. Monitoring transport and fate of de-icing salt in the roadside environment - Modelling 
and field measurements. Doctoral thesis in Land and Water Resources Engineering, 
TRITA-LWR PHD 1038. KTH Architecture and the Built Environment.

Conceptual description:
These areas are generally situated in valleys and underlain by glacial till or glaciofluvial 
deposits. The thickness of the clay layer varies between 5 and 30 m. The retention time 
of water can be long. Macropores may nevertheless be present, which enhance the 
transport of water and solutes to deeper levels. Typical depth to groundwater is 0.5-2 m. 
The clay and silt soils are usually used as arable land and are often drained.

Soil profile:
Clay 0-9 m depth
Till 9-11 m depth
Bedrock >11 m depth

Brooks and Corey parameters for clay and till represent effective parameters 
reported in Lundmark & Jansson: Generic soil descriptions for modelling water and chloride
dynamics in the unsaturated zone based on Swedish soils. Submitted December 2007 to
Geoderma. 

2008-03-17 Annika Lundmark 
